{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789484216,"nonce":"fmfswWWD+m0Iy9lqlsdG9TWWm/I=","message":"Revision: the interception point is the library's FUNCTIONS at AIR\nlevel, not only its natives, and that is what makes the value-level\nAPI free on ash. This supersedes the \"Surface\" and \"ash lowering\"\nsections above; the representation, native crate, testing and phase\nnotes stand.\n\nRayzor's `mir_wrapper` mapping keys on a class and method name; ash\nalready has that key: `types::function_keys` names every bytecode\nfunction `Class.method`, and `air_pipeline::intrinsic_of` classifies a\ncall by (lib, name) today. A table keyed on `ash.simd.Float32x4.add`,\n`.splat`, `.make`, `.get`, `.load`, `.store`, `.sum`, ... maps the\nabstract's own methods -- ordinary Haxe functions in the bytecode, whose\nbodies call the `simd` natives -- to vector operations at AIR lowering.\nOwning the source is not needed; the bytecode carries the names.\n\nHow a value of the abstract lives in AIR: as an AIR vector value\n(`ValueData.lanes`), exactly the widener's kind. An intercepted call's\noperands are either vector values already (produced by another\nintercepted call, or a phi of them) or HL pointers to the 16-byte\nrepresentation (a parameter, a field load, anything a plain call\nreturned): the latter get a `VecLoad`. An intercepted result that reaches\na non-intercepted use (stored to a field or array, passed to a plain call,\nreturned, kept across a call) is materialised there -- `alloc_bytes(16)`\nplus `VecStore` -- which is the same value the Haxe body would have\nproduced, so the two paths agree. Inside a function the chain of\noperations stays in registers with nothing allocated; that is the win\nRayzor gets from its IR type, reached from the bytecode instead of the\nsource. Phis over vector values are already legal AIR (the widener makes\nthem). `serialize` and the walker keep the calls, so the opcode paths and\nthe interpreter run the Haxe bodies and the natives; parity is the\noracle.\n\nThe low-level natives are intercepted too, as intrinsics, for hot loops\nwritten against slots directly; but the primary surface is the abstract,\nand on ash it costs what Rayzor's does.\n\nPhase 2 becomes: the interception table and the boundary\nmaterialisation pass (an escape analysis over vector values, a sibling\nof `passes/escape.rs`), then the backend forms. Phase 4 (Bytes-slot SROA)\nis no longer needed for the value abstracts.","files":null}]}